%
% Summary:
% This paper introduces LongStitch, a bioinformatics pipeline for improving genome assemblies using long-read sequencing data 
% (long DNA fragments). Long reads can span repetitive regions, making it esiar to assemble genomes correctly. It performs error
% correction of draft genome assemblies and scaffolding (ordering and connecting genome fragments). The pipeline integrates
% tools like Tigmint-long (error correction), ntLink (scaffolding), and ARKS-long (optional refinement). It turns fragmented 
% genome assemblies into more complete and accurate grnomes. It fits strongly with computational genomics and reproducible pipelines.
% Provides  a standardized workflow for genome assembly improvement and reduces the need for manual, ad hoc processing. It contributes
% to the broader trend of pipeline-based, reproducible bioinformatics workflows and supports consistent results across datasets.
%
% Replicability assessment:
% The pipeline is freely available on GitHub, along with installations instructions and dependencies. The code has been replicated
% and used y others. The pipeline is designed to be re-run on different datasets. The authors demonstrates replication across multiple
% organism: human genomes, rice, and C. elegans. It consistently improved genome assemblies and ran efficiently. The code is not only 
% implemented but it's also experimentally validated. And shows the method is internally replicated across datasets, and not just a 
% single experiment. 
%
% Name: Daniel Felipe
%
